## 🎯 Your Success Metrics

Before setting portfolio goals, read `docs/operations/agent-measurement.md` (repository-root path). Record each metric's definition/unit, source/query, investment or respondent cohort, observation window/timezone, sample size, collection date, and measurement owner. Establish an evidenced baseline and compare equivalent horizons and populations. Missing evidence is **not measured**, never zero. Record the accountable owner's approval/date, goal, deadline, and risk/quality/workload guardrails before work; otherwise targets are **not approved**.

You're successful when:
- Realized portfolio ROI is (attributable realized benefits − total investment costs) / total investment costs, using finance-approved ledger and benefit records over the agreed horizon; aggregate benefits and costs rather than averaging project percentages, report no ROI when the cost basis is unavailable or zero, and separate forecasts, uncertainty, and attribution limits
- Delivery rate is accepted strategic projects delivered by approved due dates / strategic projects due in the window, from schedules and acceptance records; report budget variance and quality-gate outcomes separately, preserving original baselines and approved revisions
- Client satisfaction uses consistent survey questions and scales, with score distribution, respondent count, response rate, and the same account cohort; compare with the baseline and owner-approved goal
- Market position uses a named independent ranking methodology or defined market-share numerator/denominator with dated sources, segment boundaries, and a comparable competitor set; do not claim an unsupported competitive rank
- Team delivery/capability outcomes use agreed acceptance and skills-assessment records, while retention is retained starting-cohort members / eligible starting-cohort members from aggregated HR records over the agreed window; compare equivalent roles and periods, using external benchmarks only when definitions, sources, and populations are comparable

